-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/09707f8e-55e0-473d-b322-ae06b6e0f5c0
-- title:
--   Cell patterns and the nogood library for 25 points in the unit square
-- statement:
--   Divide the unit square $[0,1]^2$ into the $25$ closed cells
--
--   $$Q_c=[i/5,(i+1)/5]\times[j/5,(j+1)/5],\qquad c=5i+j,\quad 0\le i,j\le 4 .$$
--
--   A cell has diameter $\sqrt2/5>1/4$, so a set of points with pairwise distances greater than $1/4$ may have two points in one cell (but not three: the two points then lie in the lower and in the upper half of the cell). This file fixes the vocabulary used to exclude such "double" cells when $25$ points are present.
--
--   1. **Cell patterns.** A *cell pattern* is a pair $(a,b)$ of sets of cells, encoded as $25$-bit masks (bit $c$ stands for $Q_c$). It asks for a point $p_{c,0}\in Q_c$ for every $c\in a$ and a second point $p_{c,1}\in Q_c$ for every $c\in b$ (`InCellR`, `layMask`).
--
--   2. **Infeasibility.** The pattern $(a,b)$ is *infeasible* (`Infeasible a b`) if there is no choice of these points with
--
--   $$|p_{c,l}-p_{c',l'}|^2>\tfrac1{16}\qquad\text{for all distinct pairs }(c,l)\ne(c',l').$$
--
--   3. **Placement.** For $k<8$ and integers $d_i,d_j$, the map `trI k di dj` sends the cell $(i,j)$ to its image under the symmetry of the $5\times5$ board coded by $k$ (bit $0$: $i\mapsto 4-i$; bit $1$: $j\mapsto 4-j$; bit $2$: exchange $i$ and $j$; applied in this order), followed by the translation by $(d_i,d_j)$ cells. `PlacedIn x k di dj A B` says that the image of the pattern $x=(a,b)$ stays on the board and is contained in the pattern $(A,B)$: cells of $a$ go to cells of $A$ and cells of $b$ to cells of $B$.
--
--   4. **The library.** `nogoods` is an explicit list of $1584$ cell patterns $(a,b)$ with $b\subseteq a$ and $b\ne\emptyset$, each with one to four double cells.
--
--   The library is the data of a computer-assisted proof that $25$ points with pairwise distances greater than $1/4$ cannot leave a cell empty: the companion theorems `twentyfive_nogood_batch1`–`twentyfive_nogood_batch6` state that every pattern of the list is infeasible, and `twentyfive_nogood_cover` states that every distribution of $25$ points over the cells with an empty cell contains a placed pattern of the list. Since infeasibility is preserved by the symmetries of the square, by translations by whole cells and by adding cells, the two facts together exclude an empty cell.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The definition file contains no proofs. The list was found by a counterexample-guided search (a SAT solver proposes a count vector avoiding all patterns found so far; an exact region-elimination procedure refutes a sub-pattern of it, which is then minimized); it is one representative per class under the $8$ symmetries and the translations. Distances are measured by `sqDist`, so "more than $1/4$ apart" reads $1/16<\mathrm{sqDist}$.
-- source:
--   Original computer-generated decomposition of CirclePackingConstants.twentyfive_missing_cell_halfbox_bound (the occupancy step, §1 of G. Wengerodt, Die dichteste Packung von 25 Kreisen in einem Quadrat, Ann. Univ. Sci. Budapest. Eötvös Sect. Math. 30 (1987) 3–15, https://annalesm.elte.hu/annales30-1987/Annales_1987_T-XXX.pdf). Method after K. J. Nurmela, P. R. J. Östergård, More optimal packings of equal circles in a square, Discrete Comput. Geom. 22 (1999) 439–457, https://doi.org/10.1007/PL00009472, §2.2–2.4 (tile combinations and region elimination), carried out here with exact integer arithmetic. The statement is not quoted from either paper.

import Definitions.Def_CirclePackingConstants

/-!
# Nogood patterns for 25 points in the unit square

The unit square is divided into the 25 closed cells of side `1/5`; cell `c = 5 i + j` (`i, j < 5`) is
`[i/5, (i+1)/5] × [j/5, (j+1)/5]`. A *cell pattern* is a pair of 25-bit masks `(a, b)`: the cells of `a`
must contain a point and the cells of `b` a second point. The pattern is *infeasible* if no such points
exist with all pairwise distances greater than `1/4`. Infeasibility is preserved when a pattern is moved by
a symmetry of the square followed by a translation by whole cells (`trI`, `PlacedIn`), and when cells are
added. `nogoods` is a list of 1584 patterns used as a library of forbidden local occupancy patterns.
-/

noncomputable section

namespace CirclePackingConstants
namespace Twentyfive

/-- `P` lies in the closed cell `c = 5 i + j` (`i = c / 5` indexes x, `j = c % 5` indexes y) of side 1/5. -/
def InCellR (P : Point) (c : ℕ) : Prop :=
  ((c / 5 : ℕ) : ℝ) / 5 ≤ P.1 ∧ P.1 ≤ (((c / 5 : ℕ) : ℝ) + 1) / 5 ∧
  ((c % 5 : ℕ) : ℝ) / 5 ≤ P.2 ∧ P.2 ≤ (((c % 5 : ℕ) : ℝ) + 1) / 5

/-- Mask of layer `l`: layer 0 uses `a` (cells with a point), layer 1 uses `b` (cells with a second point). -/
def layMask (a b l : ℕ) : ℕ := if l = 0 then a else b

/-- The cell pattern `(a, b)` (25-bit masks) admits no configuration: a point `p c 0` in every cell of `a`,
a second point `p c 1` in every cell of `b`, all these points pairwise more than 1/4 apart. -/
def Infeasible (a b : ℕ) : Prop :=
  ¬ ∃ p : ℕ → ℕ → Point,
    (∀ c l, c < 25 → l < 2 → (layMask a b l).testBit c = true → InCellR (p c l) c) ∧
    (∀ c l c' l', c < 25 → l < 2 → c' < 25 → l' < 2 → (c, l) ≠ (c', l') →
      (layMask a b l).testBit c = true → (layMask a b l').testBit c' = true →
      (1 : ℝ) / 16 < sqDist (p c l) (p c' l'))

/-- Image of cell `c` under symmetry `k < 8` (bit 0: flip i, bit 1: flip j, bit 2: swap i and j, applied in
this order) followed by the translation `(di, dj)`, as integer coordinates `(i', j')`. -/
def trI (k : ℕ) (di dj : ℤ) (c : ℕ) : ℤ × ℤ :=
  let i : ℤ := if k % 2 = 1 then 4 - (c / 5 : ℕ) else (c / 5 : ℕ)
  let j : ℤ := if (k / 2) % 2 = 1 then 4 - (c % 5 : ℕ) else (c % 5 : ℕ)
  if (k / 4) % 2 = 1 then (j + di, i + dj) else (i + di, j + dj)

/-- Every cell of the mask `m` is carried by `trI k di dj` to a cell of the board that belongs to `M`. -/
def MaskPlacedIn (k : ℕ) (di dj : ℤ) (m M : ℕ) : Prop :=
  ∀ c, c < 25 → m.testBit c = true →
    0 ≤ (trI k di dj c).1 ∧ (trI k di dj c).1 < 5 ∧ 0 ≤ (trI k di dj c).2 ∧ (trI k di dj c).2 < 5 ∧
    M.testBit (5 * (trI k di dj c).1 + (trI k di dj c).2).toNat = true

/-- The image of the pattern `x = (a, b)` under `trI k di dj` lies on the board and is contained in the
pattern `(A, B)`: cells of `a` go to cells of `A`, cells of `b` to cells of `B`. -/
def PlacedIn (x : ℕ × ℕ) (k : ℕ) (di dj : ℤ) (A B : ℕ) : Prop :=
  MaskPlacedIn k di dj x.1 A ∧ MaskPlacedIn k di dj x.2 B

